<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter III of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1239–<a href="/years/1285">1285</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Peter the Great of Aragon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter III was king of Aragon from 1276. Married to Constance, heiress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> of 1282, beginning the long Aragonese rule of the island. He also took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a> in 1284 and repelled a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against Aragon in 1285, the year he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Peter III of Aragon" width="250" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Villani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_III_rey_de_Arag%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a> and his second wife Violant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. On 13 June 1262 he married Constance II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, daughter and heiress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfred-king-of-sicily">Manfred of Sicily</a>, and he gained military experience in his father's wars against the Moors. When James died in 1276 the Crown's lands were divided: Peter received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a>, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and the Principality of Catalonia, while his younger brother became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-majorca">James II of Majorca</a>. Peter and Constance were crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in November 1276.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Peter's early years as king brought revolt in Catalonia, led by the viscount of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardona">Cardona</a> and backed by several counts, who resented his severity under his father and his failure to summon the General Court of Catalonia. In 1280 he besieged the rebels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> for a month and defeated them. Abroad, he prepared a fleet of 140 ships with 15,000 men to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a>, the uprising against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a> on 30 March 1282, a Sicilian embassy asked him to take the throne, which he claimed through Constance. He landed at Trapani on 30 August 1282 and was proclaimed king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on 4 September. Pope Martin IV excommunicated him.</p><p>His admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a> defeated the French at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Gulf of Naples</a> in 1284, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles of Salerno</a> was taken prisoner. Peter and Charles agreed to settle their quarrel by a duel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, which never took place. At home he faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-aragon">Union of Aragon</a> and was forced to grant the Privilegio General, and he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a> from the noble Juan Núñez de Lara. In 1284 Pope Martin IV granted Aragon to Charles of Valois and sanctioned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and a large French army entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> in 1285, but then lost its fleet to Roger of Lauria at Les Formigues on 4 September and was struck by dysentery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france">Philip III of France</a> died in October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Peter died at Vilafranca del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penedes">Penedès</a> in November 1285, one month after Philip III, and was buried at the Monastery of Santes Creus. In his will of 2 November 1285 he told his successor to return <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to the pope and release Angevin prisoners, but his successors ignored it: Alfonso III inherited Aragon, James II received Sicily and Frederick later succeeded James there. The kingdom was to remain a tenaciously pursued inheritance of the Aragonese royal house for five centuries. Peter was a patron of troubadours and wrote two sirventesos himself, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> placed him in Purgatory singing in accord with his former rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter III of Aragon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Drassanes of Barcelona and commanded by Roger of Lauria won a series of victories against the Angevins after <b>Peter III</b> took Sicily in 1282: off Malta in 1283, in the Bay of Naples in 1284, where Charles of Salerno was captured, and at Les Formigues in 1285, which…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1283, while <b>Peter III</b> was at war with France and the papacy over Sicily, nobles and towns organised in the Union of Aragon forced him to grant the General Privilege, and in 1287 Alfonso III yielded the still wider Privileges of the Union, until Peter IV crushed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sicilians offered their crown to <b>Peter III of Aragon</b>, husband of Manfred's daughter Constance, and Charles was left with the mainland only.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Catalan Corts, the parliament of clergy, nobility and royal towns, took definite form under <b>Peter III</b>, who in 1283 promised to summon them every year; legislation required the consent of king and Corts together, an early form of contractual government that Catalans called pactism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>At the invitation of the Sicilian rebels of the Vespers, who regarded his wife Constance as their rightful queen, <b>Peter III of Aragon</b> landed at Trapani.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It also sought a diplomatic solution to the conquest of Sicily by <b>Peter III of Aragon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-20">20 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Vilafranca del Penedès, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-0.38&amp;year=1268" aria-label="Valencia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4541 750 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter III of Aragon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4991 1020h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4991 1020h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-0.38&amp;year=1268">What was here in 1268? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
